Consider the following two typed calculi, each equipped with a semantic notion of subtyping (i.e., subtyping defined as set-theoretic containment of the denotations of types, where types are interpreted as sets of values).

(1) Duce is a call-by-value λ-calculus extended with overloaded (intersection-typed) functions and recursive types. Its types are built from basic types, products, and arrow types using the boolean connectives ∨, ∧, ¬, and a recursion operator μ. Subtyping t ≤ s is defined semantically: t ≤ s iff every value belonging to t belongs to s. In particular, an overloaded function with type ⋀_{i∈I}(t_i → s_i) accepts arguments in ⋁_i t_i and dispatches according to the most specific arrow.

(2) The local π-calculus π is the asynchronous π-calculus with a type system featuring input/output channel-capability types ch^+(t) (output-only), ch^−(t) (input-only), and ch(t) (both), together with the same boolean and recursive type constructors as Duce, and semantic subtyping on these types. In the local discipline, only the output capability of a channel can be transmitted, so received channels carry only ch^+(t) types.

Problem. Define a translation ⟦·⟧ from Duce terms to π processes and a translation |·| from Duce types to π types that is:
 (a) type-preserving: if Γ ⊢ M : t in Duce, then ⟦Γ⟧ ⊢ ⟦M⟧ : |t| in π (under a suitable continuation-passing convention);
 (b) subtyping-preserving: t ≤_Duce s implies |t| ≤_π |s|;
 (c) sound for overloaded functions: the dispatch semantics of intersection-typed Duce functions is faithfully simulated by the π processes;
 (d) compatible with recursive types under the translation; and
 (e) reconciles the "channel-type gap" between the natural Duce arrow translation, which would require a fully bidirectional channel type ch^λ(t) for the function-argument channel, and the local-π discipline, which only provides ch^−(t) on the receiving side.

Either exhibit such an encoding or prove that no encoding satisfying (a)–(e) exists, in which case formulate and develop instead an integrated concurrent extension of Duce (a single calculus combining higher-order functional computation with overloading and recursive types together with π-style concurrency) in which the desired typing and subtyping properties hold natively.

